static func harness_running() -> bool:
	for v in HARNESS_VARS:
		if OS.get_environment(String(v)) != "":
			return true
	return false


# --- economy pacing target (PYXIS_PACINGTEST) -------------------------------
# The eight-minute "standard loop" the economy is tuned against, as an activity
# budget rather than a wall-clock replay. Two reasons it is not a literal eight
# real-time minutes: the one instrument we tune the economy with would be too
# slow to iterate on, and Engine.time_scale would wreck every mover in the game,
# because this codebase integrates positions by hand (see obstacles.gd) instead
# of using physics. So the harness plays each activity FOR REAL with a starter
# fit and measures what the game actually pays for it — only these weights are
# authored, every yield below them is observed.
const PACING_LOOP := {"swarm": 14, "sentry": 5, "derelict": 1, "trade": 1,
	"contract": 1, "survey": 2}
# The prospector's session: a SECOND authored weighting folded over the SAME
# measured yields, never a second set of measurements. That is the whole design
# of it — two profiles that share one `yields` dictionary cannot drift apart by
# valuing an activity differently, so a parity failure always means the ROUTES
# diverged and never that the instrument did.
#
# Note what is NOT here: PACING_LOOP carries no "seam" term and is untouched by
# mining. Mining is settled as an ALTERNATIVE loop rather than extra income, and
# the standard loop already sits at ~97% of its ceiling — there is no room for a
# seventh earner and none is taken. A prospector trades combat away for cutting
# and lands beside the recurring rate, not above it.
#
# The tuning knob is the weight "seam": 8 — how much of a session cutting fills.
# Never an ore price: a price moves the market, the haul_ore contract, the
# interdiction weight and four port inventories at once, and this weight moves
# only this.
const PACING_PROSPECT := {"swarm": 4, "sentry": 2, "seam": 8, "trade": 1,
	"contract": 1, "survey": 1}
# How far the prospector's route may sit from the recurring standard rate. Wide
# on purpose: this is checking that mining is a ROUTE rather than a raise or a
# trap, not that two playstyles pay to the credit.
const PACING_ROUTE_TOLERANCE := 0.20
const PACING_MINUTES := 8.0
# CEILING: earned-per-loop as a fraction of the whole catalog. Above this the
# catalog is a shopping list again, which was the phase-1 problem.
const PACING_MAX_RATIO := 0.15
# FLOOR (and a sanity ceiling): the design's plain-language target, that a loop
# is worth roughly one mid-tier module. Lance Array is the reference — the first
# real "I chose this" purchase.
#
# Deliberately NOT a catalog fraction. Adding the six Mk II tiers in B5 grew the
# catalog 84% and would have "failed" a catalog-fraction floor without a single
# earning changing — the metric would have been reporting on how much content
# exists, not on how the game paces. Modules-per-loop holds still while the
# catalog grows, which is the property a pacing assertion needs.
const PACING_MIN_MODULES := 0.60
const PACING_MAX_MODULES := 3.00
# v22: RIG_STRIKE, NOT LANCE_1 — §41's re-derivation, paid at last (§86). The
# Lance Array was "the first real I-chose-this purchase" when it was named, and
# the Mk II tiers then grew the catalog 84% and moved the middle out from under
# it: measured 2026-08-18, lance_1's 5,800 sat at the 18TH PERCENTILE of 39
# priced modules against a median of 14,000. The steady loop read 1.91x
# "mid-tier" while actually buying four-fifths of a median module — the economy
# was on target and the YARDSTICK was lying short. Strike Frame is today's
# version of the same idea (a frame is a build identity, not an upgrade), sits
# beside the median at 14,500, and carries the same power draw, so MINING COSTS
# A MOUNT measures exactly what it measured before. THE YARDSTICK IS MID-TIER
# keeps this paid: the reference must sit in the middle third of the priced
# catalog, asserted against the live module list, so the next 84% of growth
# turns a leg red instead of quietly shortening the ruler for another year.
# How far the ship may move on the attract screen while a bearing key is held.
# NOT a drift tolerance in spirit -- _enter_attract disables input and parks the
# hull, so the honest expectation is zero and this only absorbs physics settling.
# The failure it is sized against is not subtle: a ship that actually flew would
# cover hundreds of units in the second the leg holds the key.
const TITLE_DRIFT_MAX := 0.5

const PACING_MIDTIER := "rig_strike"

