# --- THE MUSTER (v18, stage 4) -----------------------------------------------
# A massed swarm gathers 75u outside the charted volume and marches in; the
# Cordon amasses at the chart edge and holds the line there.
#
# THE FICTION RESOLVES THE CONSTITUTIONAL PROBLEM. The band-1 beacon has
# promised since v13 that "security patrols do not operate beyond this marker",
# DEEPTEST pins the words, and stage 2a turned them into an assertion about
# ships. So the Cordon does NOT sally out to meet the column: it forms up ON the
# marker and waits. A player can fly out and meet the column early — alone, past
# the line, exactly as the beacon warned — or stand with the Cordon. The event
# teaches the game's territorial rule instead of bending it.
#
# The column comes down the SURVEY HULL'S BEARING, which is already "the wound"
# in each system: the direction the derelict drifted, the direction the unlock
# hint sends you, and now the direction things come from.
const MUSTER_ROLL_INTERVAL := 300.0
const MUSTER_CHANCE := 0.25
# Minimum spacing, independent of the roll. At a 25% chance every five minutes
# the gap between musters is geometric and can be two rolls; a hard floor stops
# the rare double from reading as a bug.
# --- the Cordon re-forms (v18, playtest round 4) -----------------------------
# How long between one picket returning to its beat and the next. The LINE
# BROKEN broadcast has promised "expect elevated activity until we can re-form"
# since stage 4 and nothing implemented it; §51 recorded that sentries do not
# respawn and that this was fine "because clearing a post is work the player
# did", which is true of the DEPLETION and says nothing about the RECOVERY. A
# playtest found the gap from the other side: charted space only ever gets
# emptier, which is backwards for a volume that is supposed to feel patrolled.
#
# 120s per ship, capped at the routes the system authored, so a fully cleared
# Cordon takes four to eight minutes to stand back up — long enough that killing
# one is still a thing you did, short enough to be visible inside a session. It
# is deliberately slower than PACING_MINUTES (8.0) is long, so a player cannot
# treat the beat as a queue.
const CORDON_REFORM_INTERVAL := 120.0

# --- colony jumps (v20, Hunt stage 6) ----------------------------------------
# THE COOLDOWN IS THE MECHANISM, AND THE FEE IS NOT. Worth stating plainly
# because the obvious reading is backwards: recall home is FREE and uncapped
# (_recall_sequence says why), so a colony jump does not save a one-way trip,
# it halves a round one. The deepest instrument sits ~4,200u out, which is
# ~730s of flying at the hull's 5.75 u/s — and 500 credits amortised over a
# five-minute cooldown is 100 cr/min, a rounding error against the loop. Only
# the cooldown bounds how often the deep can be re-entered.
#
# ONE STAMP FOR ALL OF THEM, on GameState.last_outbound_jump. Per-colony stamps
# would make founding five colonies mean five times the outbound frequency,
# which is the farm this whole clause exists to prevent — and it is the one
# defect a single-colony test cannot see, so ONE CLOCK FOR ALL OF THEM stages
# two instruments and jumps between them.
const COLONY_JUMP_FEE := 500
const COLONY_JUMP_COOLDOWN := 300.0

